WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
In a moment I'm going to tell you about a certain&nbsp;
really nice puzzle involving the shadow of a cube.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.880
But before we get to that I should say that the&nbsp;
point of this video is not exactly the puzzle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.000
per se, it's about two distinct problem-solving&nbsp;
styles that are reflected in two different ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.680
that we can tackle this problem. In fact&nbsp;
let's anthropomorphize those two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.640
styles by imagining two students, Alice and&nbsp;
Bob who embody each one of the approaches.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.080
So Bob will be the kind of student who really&nbsp;
loves calculation. As soon as there's a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.400
when he can dig into the details and get a very&nbsp;
concrete view of the concrete situation in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.200
of him, that's where he's the most pleased.&nbsp;
Alice on the other hand is more inclined to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.680
procrastinate the computations, not because she&nbsp;
doesn't know how to do them or doesn't want to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.200
per se, but she prefers to get a nice high-level&nbsp;
general overview of the kind of problem she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.280
dealing with, the general shape that it has,&nbsp;
before she digs into the computations themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.240
She's most pleased if she understands not just&nbsp;
the specific question sitting in front of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.840
but also the broadest possible&nbsp;
way that you could generalize it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.040
and especially if the more general&nbsp;
view can lend itself to more swift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:11.840
and elegant computations once she does&nbsp;
actually sit down to carry them out.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.840
Now the puzzle that both of them are going to&nbsp;
be faced with is to find the average area for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.280
the shadow of a cube. So if I have a cube kind of&nbsp;
sitting here hovering in space, there are, a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.160
things that influence the area of its shadow. One&nbsp;
obvious one would be the size of the cube, smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.120
cube smaller shadow. But also if it's sitting&nbsp;
at different orientations, those orientations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.760
correspond to different particular shadows with&nbsp;
different areas. And when I say find the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.960
here, what I mean is the average over all possible&nbsp;
orientations for a particular size of the cube.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.240
The astute among you might point out that it&nbsp;
also matters a lot where the light source is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.200
If the light source were very low close to the&nbsp;
cube itself then the shadow ends up larger and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.280
the light source were kind of positioned laterally&nbsp;
off to the side, this can distort the shadow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.440
give it a very different shape. Accounting&nbsp;
for that light position stands to be highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.920
interesting in its own right, but the puzzle is&nbsp;
hard enough as it is, so at least initially let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.520
do the easiest thing we can and say that the light&nbsp;
is directly above the cube and really far away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.640
Effectively infinitely far so that all we're&nbsp;
considering is a flat projection, in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.840
that if you look at any coordinates (x, y, z)&nbsp;
in space the flat projection would be (x, y, 0)

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.640
So just to get our bearings, the easiest&nbsp;
situation to think about would be if the cube is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.760
straight up with two of its faces parallel to the&nbsp;
ground. In that case this flat projection shadow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.280
is simply a square, and if we say the side lengths&nbsp;
of the cube are s, then the area of that shadow is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.080
s squared. And by the way, anytime that I have&nbsp;
a label up on these animations like the one down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.120
here I'll be assuming that the relevant cube&nbsp;
has a side length of 1. Another special case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.000
among all the orientations that's fun to think&nbsp;
about is if the long diagonal is parallel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.400
the direction of the light. In that case the&nbsp;
shadow actually looks like a regular hexagon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.880
and if you use some of the methods that we will&nbsp;
develop in a few minutes, you can compute that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.760
area of that shadow is exactly the square root of&nbsp;
three times the area of one of the square faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.680
But of course more often the actual shadow will&nbsp;
not be so regular as a square or a hexagon, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.160
some harder-to-think-about shape based on some&nbsp;
harder-to-think-about orientation for this cube.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.920
Earlier, I casually threw out this phrase&nbsp;
of averaging over all possible orientations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.640
but you could rightly ask what exactly is&nbsp;
that supposed to mean. I think a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.640
us have an intuitive feel for what we want&nbsp;
it to mean, at least in the sense of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.240
experiment would you do to verify it. You might&nbsp;
imagine tossing this cube in the air like a die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.440
freezing it at some arbitrary point, recording&nbsp;
the area of the shadow from that position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.680
and then repeating. If you do this many many&nbsp;
times over and over you can take the mean of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.800
your sample. The number that we want to get at,&nbsp;
the true average here, should be whatever that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.840
experimental mean approaches as you do more&nbsp;
and more tosses approaching infinitely many.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.720
Even still, the sticklers among you could complain&nbsp;
that doesn't really answer the question, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.520
it leaves open the issue of how we're defining&nbsp;
a "random" toss. The proper way to answer this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:15.120
if we want it to be more formal, would be to first&nbsp;
describe the space of all possible orientations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.960
which mathematicians have actually&nbsp;
given a fancy name. They call it SO(3),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.680
typically defined in terms of a certain family&nbsp;
of 3-by-3 matrices. And the question we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.840
to answer is "What probability distribution are we&nbsp;
putting to this entire space?" It's only when such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.480
a probability distribution is well-defined that&nbsp;
we can answer a question involving an average.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.000
If you are a stickler for that kind of thing, I&nbsp;
want you to hold off on that question until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.320
end of the video. You'll be surprised at how far&nbsp;
we can get with the more heuristic experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.560
idea of just repeating a bunch of random tosses&nbsp;
without really defining the distribution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.960
Once we see Alice and Bob's solutions,&nbsp;
it's actually very interesting to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:56.320
how exactly each one of them defined&nbsp;
this distribution along their way.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.600
And remember, this is not meant to be&nbsp;
a lesson about cube shadows, per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.080
but a lesson about problem-solving told through&nbsp;
the lens of two different mindsets that we might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.640
bring to the puzzle. And as with any lesson&nbsp;
on problem-solving, the goal here is not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.880
get to the answer as quickly as we can, but&nbsp;
hopefully for you to feel like you found the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.400
answer yourself. So if ever there's a point&nbsp;
when you feel like you might have an idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.840
give yourself the freedom to&nbsp;
pause and try to think it through.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.680
As a first step, and this is really independent&nbsp;
of any particular problem-solving styles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.840
just anytime you find a hard question, a&nbsp;
good thing that you can do is ask "What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.400
the simplest possible non-trivial variant&nbsp;
of the problem that you can try to solve?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.840
In our case what you might say is, okay, let's&nbsp;
forget about averaging over all the orientations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.680
That's a tricky thing to think about. And let's&nbsp;
even forget about all the different faces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.480
the cube, because they overlap and that's also&nbsp;
tricky to think about. Just for one particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.880
face and one particular orientation,&nbsp;
can we compute the area of this shadow?

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.840
Once more, if you want to get your bearings with&nbsp;
some special cases the easiest is when that face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.000
is parallel to the ground in which case the&nbsp;
area of the shadow is the same as the area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.200
of the face. And on the other hand if we were to&nbsp;
tilt that face 90-degrees, then its shadow will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.480
be a straight line and it has an area of zero. So&nbsp;
Bob looks at this and he wants an actual formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.840
for that shadow, and the way he might think about&nbsp;
it is to consider the normal vector perpendicular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.960
off of that face. What seems relevant is the angle&nbsp;
that that normal vector makes with the vertical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.920
with the direction where the light is&nbsp;
coming from, which we might call theta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.240
Now from the two special cases we just looked at,&nbsp;
we know that when theta is equal to 0, the area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.960
that shadow is the same as the area of the shape&nbsp;
itself, which is s squared if the square has side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.560
lengths s. And if theta is equal to 90 degrees,&nbsp;
then the area of that shadow is zero. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.800
probably not too hard to guess that trigonometry&nbsp;
will be somehow relevant, so anyone comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.880
with their trig functions could probably&nbsp;
hazard a guess as to what the right formula is.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.600
But Bob is more detail-oriented than that. He&nbsp;
wants to properly prove what that area should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.600
rather than just making a guess based on the&nbsp;
endpoints. The way you might think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.000
could be something like this. If we consider&nbsp;
the plane that passes through the vertical as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.880
well as our normal vector, and then we consider&nbsp;
all the different slices of our shape that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.920
in that plane, or parallel to that plane, then&nbsp;
we can focus our attention on a two-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.560
variant of the problem. If we just look at one&nbsp;
of those slices, who has a normal vector an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.840
angle theta away from the vertical, its shadow&nbsp;
might look something like this. And if we draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.920
a vertical line up to the left here, we have&nbsp;
ourselves a right triangle. And from here we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.400
can do a little bit of angle chasing, where we&nbsp;
follow around what that angle theta implies about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:42.080
the rest of the diagram. And this means the lower&nbsp;
right angle in this triangle is precisely theta.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.640
So when we want to understand the size of this&nbsp;
shadow in comparison to the original size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.760
the piece, we can think about the cosine of that&nbsp;
angle theta, which remember is the adjacent over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.080
the hypotenuse. It's literally the ratio between&nbsp;
the size of the shadow and the size of the slice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.240
So the factor by which the slice gets squished&nbsp;
down in this direction is exactly cosine of theta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.280
And if we broaden our view to the entire square&nbsp;
all the slices in that direction get scaled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.320
the same factor. But in the other direction,&nbsp;
the one perpendicular to that slice, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:19.760
no stretching or squishing because the face is&nbsp;
not at all tilted in that direction. So overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.560
the two-dimensional shadow of our two-dimensional&nbsp;
face should also be scaled down by this factor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.800
a cosine of theta. It lines up with what you&nbsp;
might intuitively guess given the case where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.200
the angle is 0 degrees and the case where it's 90&nbsp;
degrees, but it's reassuring to see why it's true

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.440
Actually, as stated so far, this is not&nbsp;
quite correct. There is a small problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.000
with the formula that we've written. In the&nbsp;
case where theta is bigger than 90 degrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.520
the cosine would actually come out to be&nbsp;
negative, but of course we don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.920
consider the shadow to have negative area. At&nbsp;
least not in a problem like this. So there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.960
two different ways you could solve this. You&nbsp;
could say we only ever want to consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.000
normal vector that is pointing up, that has a&nbsp;
positive z component. Or more simply we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.560
say just take the absolute value of that&nbsp;
cosine, and that gives us a valid formula.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.800
So bob's happy because he has a precise&nbsp;
formula describing the area of the shadow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.400
but Alice starts to think about it a little bit&nbsp;
differently. She says, okay we've got some shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:21.520
and then we apply a rotation that sort of situates&nbsp;
it into 3d space in some way, and then we apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.000
a flat projection that shoves that back into&nbsp;
two-dimensional space. And what stands out to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.880
is that both of these are linear transformations.&nbsp;
That means that in principle you could describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:35.200
each one of them with a matrix, and that the&nbsp;
overall transformation would look like the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.240
of those two matrices. What Alice knows from&nbsp;
one of her favorite subjects, linear algebra, is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.560
that if you take some shape and you consider its&nbsp;
area, then you apply some linear transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.880
the area of that output looks like some constant&nbsp;
times the original area of the shape. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.960
specifically we have a name for that constant,&nbsp;
it's called the determinant of the transformation.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.160
If you're not so comfortable with linear algebra,&nbsp;
we could give a much more intuitive description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.360
and say if you uniformly stretch the original&nbsp;
shape in some direction, the output will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.400
uniformly get stretched in some direction. So the&nbsp;
area of each of them should scale in proportion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.520
to each other. Now, in principle Alice could&nbsp;
compute this determinant. But it's not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.000
her style to do that, at least not to do so&nbsp;
immediately. Instead the thing that she writes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.920
down is how this proportionality constant between&nbsp;
our original shape and its shadow does not depend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.960
on the original shape. We could be talking about&nbsp;
the shadow of this cat outline, or anything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:35.600
and the size of it doesn't really matter, the&nbsp;
only thing affecting that proportionality constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.000
is what transformation we're applying, which in&nbsp;
this context means we could write it down as some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.600
factor that depends on the rotation being applied&nbsp;
to the shape. In the back of our mind because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.680
bob's calculation we know what that factor looks&nbsp;
like, you know it's the absolute value of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.320
cosine of the angle between the normal vector and&nbsp;
the vertical. But Alice right now is just saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:58.000
"yeah, yeah, I can think about that eventually&nbsp;
when I want to." But she knows we're about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.160
average over all the different orientations&nbsp;
anyway, so she holds out some hope that any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.640
specific formula about a specific orientation&nbsp;
might get washed away in that average.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.600
Now it's easy to look at this and say, "Okay,&nbsp;
well Alice isn't really doing anything then!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.680
Of course the area of the shadow is&nbsp;
proportional to the area of the original shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.920
they're both two-dimensional quantities, they&nbsp;
should both scale like two-dimensional things.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.920
But keep in mind this would not at all be&nbsp;
true if we were dealing with the harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.320
case that has a closer light source. In&nbsp;
that case the projection is not linear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:32.960
So for example if I rotate this cat so that its&nbsp;
tail ends up quite close to the light source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.320
then if I stretch the original&nbsp;
shape uniformly in the x direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.920
say by a factor of 1.5, it might have a very&nbsp;
disproportionate effect on the ultimate shadow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:46.000
because the tail gets very disproportionately&nbsp;
blown up as it gets really close to the light.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.800
Again, Alice is keeping an eye out for what&nbsp;
properties of the problem are actually relevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.320
because that helps her know how much she&nbsp;
can generalize things. Does the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:59.200
we're thinking about a square face and not&nbsp;
some other shape matter? No not really. Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.720
the fact that the transformation&nbsp;
is linear matter? Yes, absolutely.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.640
Alice can also apply a similar way of&nbsp;
thinking about the average shadow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:16.320
any shape like this. Say we have some sequence&nbsp;
of rotations that we apply to our square face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:22.640
and let's call them R1, R2, R3, and so on. Then&nbsp;
the area of the shadow in each one of those cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.200
looks like some factor times the area of the&nbsp;
square, and that factor depends on the rotation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.200
So if we take an empirical average&nbsp;
for that shadow across the sample of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.960
rotations we're looking at right now,&nbsp;
the way it looks is to add up all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:38.160
those shadow areas and then divide&nbsp;
by the total number that we have.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:44.960
Now, because of the linearity, this area of the&nbsp;
original square can cleanly factor out of all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:49.520
that, and it ends up on the left. This isn't the&nbsp;
exact average that we're looking for, it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:54.480
an empirical mean of a sample of rotations. But&nbsp;
in principle what we're looking for is what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:59.440
approaches as the size of our sample approaches&nbsp;
infinity. And all the parts that depend on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:05.120
size of the sample sit cleanly away from the area&nbsp;
itself. So whatever this approaches, in the limit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:08.880
it's just going to be some number. It might be&nbsp;
a royal pain to compute, we're not sure about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.520
that yet, but the thing that Alice notes is that&nbsp;
it's independent of the size and the shape of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:19.280
particular 2d thing that we're looking at. It's a&nbsp;
universal proportionality constant, and her hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:24.400
is that that universality somehow lends itself&nbsp;
to a more elegant way to deduce what it must be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.440
Now Bob would be eager to compute&nbsp;
this constant here and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:33.120
and in a few minutes I'll show you how he&nbsp;
does it. But before that, I do want to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.640
in Alice's world for a little bit more, because&nbsp;
this is where things start to really get fun.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:45.120
In her desire to understand the overall structure&nbsp;
of the question before diving into the details,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.080
she's curious now about how the area of the&nbsp;
shadow of the cube relates to the area of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:55.280
individual faces. If we can say something about&nbsp;
the average area of a particular face, does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:00.240
tell us anything about the average area of the&nbsp;
cube as a whole? For example, a simple thing we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:05.680
could say is that that area is definitely less&nbsp;
than the sum of the areas across all the faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:11.040
because there's a meaningful amount of overlap&nbsp;
between those shadows. But it's not entirely clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.000
how to think about that overlap, because if we&nbsp;
focus our attention just on two particular faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:20.160
in some orientations they don't overlap at&nbsp;
all, but in other orientations they do have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.360
some overlap. The specific shape and area of that&nbsp;
overlap seems a little bit tricky to think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.840
much less how on earth we would average that&nbsp;
across all of the different orientations.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:34.080
But Alice has about three clever&nbsp;
insights through this whole problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:38.960
and this is the first one of them. She says,&nbsp;
actually, if we think about the whole cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:44.320
not just a pair of faces, we can conclude that&nbsp;
the area of the shadow for a given orientation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:48.000
is exactly one-half the sum of&nbsp;
the areas of all of the faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:53.760
Intuitively, you can maybe guess that half of them&nbsp;
are bathed in the light, and half of them are not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.160
But here's the way that she justifies it.&nbsp;
She says for a particular ray of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:03.040
they would go from the sky and eventually hit a&nbsp;
point in the shadow, that ray passes through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:08.000
cube at exactly two points. There's one moment&nbsp;
when it enters, and one moment when it exits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:15.200
so every point in that shadow corresponds to&nbsp;
exactly two faces above it. Well, okay, that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:19.680
exactly true. If that beam of light happened to go&nbsp;
through the edge of one of the squares, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:24.720
little bit of ambiguity on how many faces it's&nbsp;
passing. But those account for zero area inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:28.880
the shadow, so we're safe to ignore them if the&nbsp;
thing we're trying to do is compute the area.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:35.840
If Alice is pressed and she needs to justify&nbsp;
why exactly this is true, which is important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.920
for understanding how the problem might&nbsp;
generalize, she can appeal to the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:44.560
convexity. Convexity is one of those properties&nbsp;
where a lot of us have an intuitive sense for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:48.880
what it should mean. You know, it's shapes&nbsp;
that just bulge out, they never dent inward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:52.960
But mathematicians have a pretty clever way of&nbsp;
formalizing it that's helpful for actual proofs

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:59.120
They say that a set is "convex" if the line&nbsp;
that connects any two points inside that set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:05.040
is entirely contained within the set itself. So a&nbsp;
square is convex because no matter where you put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:10.000
two points inside that square, the line connecting&nbsp;
them is entirely contained inside the square.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.640
But something like the symbol pi is not&nbsp;
convex. I can easily find two different points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:19.600
so that the line connecting them has to peak&nbsp;
outside of the set itself. None of the letters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:25.040
in the word "convex" are themselves convex. You&nbsp;
can find two points so that the line connecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:30.240
them has to pass outside of the set. It's a really&nbsp;
clever way to formalize this idea of a shape that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:35.760
only bulges out, because any time that it dents&nbsp;
inward, you can find these counter-example lines

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:41.200
For our cube, because it's convex, between the&nbsp;
first point of entry and the last point of exit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:46.240
it has to stay entirely inside the cube, by the&nbsp;
definition of convexity. But if we were dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:51.200
with some other non-convex shape, like a donut,&nbsp;
you could find a ray of light that enters then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:56.400
exits then enters and exits again. So you wouldn't&nbsp;
have a clean two-to-one cover from the shadows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:00.640
The shadows of all of its different parts, if&nbsp;
you were to cover this in a bunch of faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:03.840
would not be precisely two times&nbsp;
the area of the shadow itself.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.560
So that's the first key insight, the face&nbsp;
shadows double-cover the cube shadow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.840
And the next one is a little bit more&nbsp;
symbolic, so let's start things off by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:16.320
abbreviating our notation a little to make room on&nbsp;
the screen. Instead of writing Area(Shadow(Cube)),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:23.520
I'm just going to write S(Cube). And similarly&nbsp;
instead of Area(Shadow(a particular face)), I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.240
just going to write S(F_j), where that subscript&nbsp;
j indicates which face I'm talking about.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:33.200
But of course, we should really be talking about&nbsp;
the shadow of a particular rotation applied to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:38.480
cube, so I might write this as S of some rotation&nbsp;
applied to the cube. And likewise on the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.600
it's the area of the shadow of that same&nbsp;
rotation applied to a given one of the faces.

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:49.200
With the more compact notation at hand, let's&nbsp;
think about the average of this shadow area across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:55.840
many different rotations, some sample of R1, R2,&nbsp;
R3, and so on. Again, that average just involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:00.960
adding up all of those shadow areas and then&nbsp;
dividing them by n, and in principle if we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.360
to look at this for larger and larger samples,&nbsp;
letting n approach infinity, that would give us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:09.920
the average area of the shadow of the cube. Some&nbsp;
of you might be thinking, "yes, we know this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:14.240
you've said this already." But it's beneficial to&nbsp;
write it out so that we can understand why it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:19.280
that expressing the shadow area for a particular&nbsp;
rotation of the cube as a sum across all of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:24.400
faces, or one half times that sum at least...why&nbsp;
is that beneficial? What is that going to do for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:28.800
us? Well, let's just write it out, where for&nbsp;
each one of these rotations of the cube we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:34.480
could break down that shadow as a sum across that&nbsp;
same rotation applied across all of the faces. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:38.960
when it's written as a grid like this, we can get&nbsp;
to Alice's second insight, which is to shift the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:43.600
way that we're thinking about the sum from going&nbsp;
row-by-row to instead going column-by-column.

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:50.160
For example if we focused our attention just&nbsp;
on the first column, what it's telling us is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:55.680
to add up the area of the shadow of the first&nbsp;
face across many different orientations. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:00.320
we were to take that sum and divide it by the&nbsp;
size of our sample, that gives us an empirical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:05.360
average for the area of the shadow of this&nbsp;
face. So if we take larger and larger samples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:09.840
letting that size go to infinity, this will&nbsp;
approach the average shadow area for a square.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.960
Likewise, the second column&nbsp;
can be thought of as telling us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:20.080
the average area for the second face of the&nbsp;
cube, which should of course be the same number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:24.720
And same deal for any other column, it's telling&nbsp;
us the average area for a particular face.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:28.320
So that gives us a very different way&nbsp;
of thinking about our whole expression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:32.320
Instead of saying add up the areas of the&nbsp;
cubes at all the different orientations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:36.000
we could say just add up the average&nbsp;
shadows for the six different faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.080
and multiply the total by one half. The term&nbsp;
on the left here is thinking about adding up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.600
rows first, and the term on the right is&nbsp;
thinking about adding up columns first.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.840
In short, the average of&nbsp;
the sum of the face shadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:53.120
is the same as the sum of the average of the&nbsp;
face shadows. Maybe that swap seems simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.000
maybe it doesn't, but I can tell you&nbsp;
that there is actually a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.600
more than meets the eye to the step that&nbsp;
we just took. But we'll get to that later.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:03.840
And remember, we know that the&nbsp;
average area for a particular face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.480
looks like some universal proportionality&nbsp;
constant times the area of that face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:12.800
so if we're adding this up across all the faces&nbsp;
of the cube, we could think of this as equaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:16.800
some constant times the surface area of&nbsp;
the cube. And that's pretty interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:21.360
the average area for the shadow of this cube is&nbsp;
going to be proportional to its surface area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:26.720
But at the same time, you might complain, "Well&nbsp;
Alice is just pushing around a bunch of symbols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:30.960
here, because none of this matters if we don't&nbsp;
know what that proportionality constant is!"

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:36.720
I mean it almost seems obvious. Like, of course&nbsp;
the average shadow area should be proportional to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:41.360
the surface area, they're both two-dimensional&nbsp;
quantities, so they should scale in lock step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:46.640
with each other. It's not obvious. After all,&nbsp;
for a closer light source it simply wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:52.240
be true. And also this business where we added&nbsp;
up the grid column-by-column versus row-by-row&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:56.320
is a little more nuanced than it might&nbsp;
look at first, there's a subtle hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.320 --> 00:21:00.800
assumption underlying all of this which carries&nbsp;
a special significance when we choose to revisit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:06.160
the question of what probability distribution is&nbsp;
being taken across the space of all orientations.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:11.600
But more than anything, the reason that it's&nbsp;
not obvious is that the significance of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:17.040
result right here is not merely that these two&nbsp;
values are proportional. It's that an analogous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:22.880
fact will hold true for any convex solids, and&nbsp;
crucially, the actual content of what Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.760
has built up so far is that it'll be the same&nbsp;
proportionality constant across all of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:32.640
Now if you really mull over that, some of you&nbsp;
may be able to predict the way that Alice is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:36.880
able to finish things off from here. It's really&nbsp;
delightful, it's honestly my main reason for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:42.240
covering this topic. But before we get into it,&nbsp;
I think it's easy to under-appreciate her result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:46.000
unless we dig into the details of&nbsp;
what it is that she manages to avoid.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.920
So let's take a moment to turn our&nbsp;
attention back into Bob's world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:55.360
because while Alice has been doing all of this,&nbsp;
he's been busy doing some computations. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.360 --> 00:22:00.240
what he's been working on is finding exactly&nbsp;
what Alice has yet to figure out, which is how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:04.880
take the formula that he found for the area of a&nbsp;
square's shadow, and taking the natural next step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:11.840
of trying to find the average of that square's&nbsp;
shadow, averaged over all possible orientations.

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:19.040
The way Bob starts, if he's thinking about all the&nbsp;
different possible orientations for this square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:23.120
is to ask what are all the different normal&nbsp;
vectors that that square can have in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:27.600
these orientations. Because everything about&nbsp;
its shadow comes down to that normal vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:32.480
It's not too hard to see that all those possible&nbsp;
normal vectors trace out the surface of a sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:37.440
If we assume it's a unit normal vector, it's a&nbsp;
sphere with radius 1. And furthermore, Bob figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:42.560
that each point of the sphere should be just as&nbsp;
likely to occur as any other, our probabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:46.240
should be uniform in that way. There's no&nbsp;
reason to prefer one direction over another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:51.200
But in the context of continuous probabilities&nbsp;
it's not very helpful to talk about the likelihood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:55.680
of a particular individual point, because in the&nbsp;
uncountable infinity of points on the sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:01.200
that would be zero and unhelpful. So instead&nbsp;
the more precise way to phrase this uniformity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:05.840
would be to say the probability that our normal&nbsp;
vector lands in any given patch of area on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:11.840
sphere should be proportional to that area itself.&nbsp;
More specifically it should equal the area of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:16.800
little patch divided by the total surface area&nbsp;
of the sphere. If that's true no matter what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.040
patch of area we're considering, that's what we&nbsp;
mean by a uniform distribution on the sphere.

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:26.320
Now to be clear, points on the sphere are not&nbsp;
the same thing as orientations in 3d space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:29.840
because even if you know what normal&nbsp;
vector the square is going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:34.080
that leaves us with another degree of freedom. The&nbsp;
square could be rotated about that normal vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:39.200
but Bob doesn't actually have to care about that&nbsp;
extra degree of freedom, because in all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:44.960
cases the area of the shadow is the same. It's&nbsp;
only dependent on the cosine of the angle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:49.360
that normal vector and the vertical, which is&nbsp;
kind of neat. All those shadows are genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:53.440
different shapes, they're not the same, but&nbsp;
the area of each of them will be the same.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:58.560
What this means is that when Bob wants&nbsp;
this average shadow area over all possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:03.680
orientations, all he really needs to know is the&nbsp;
average value of this absolute value of cosine of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:08.880
theta for all different possible normal vectors,&nbsp;
all different possible points on the sphere.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:13.920
So how do you compute an average like this?&nbsp;
Well if we lived in some kind of discrete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:18.320
pixelated world, where there's only a finite&nbsp;
number of possible angles theta that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:23.120
normal vector could have, the average would be&nbsp;
pretty straightforward. What you do is find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:27.840
probability of landing on any particular value of&nbsp;
theta, which will tell us something like how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:32.480
of the sphere do normal vectors with that angle&nbsp;
make up, and then you multiply it by the thing we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:37.920
want to take the average of, this formula for the&nbsp;
area of the shadow. And then you would add that up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:43.840
over all of the different possible values of theta&nbsp;
ranging from 0 up to 180 degrees, or pi radians.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:48.880
But of course in reality there is a&nbsp;
continuum of possible values of theta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:54.880
this uncountable infinity, and the probability of&nbsp;
landing on any specific particular value of theta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.880 --> 00:25:00.240
will actually be zero, and so a sum like this&nbsp;
unfortunately doesn't really make any sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:04.080
Or if it does make sense, adding up infinitely&nbsp;
many zeros should just give us a zero.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:10.400
The short answer for what we do instead is that&nbsp;
we compute an integral. And I'll level with you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:14.080
the hard part here is I'm not entirely sure what&nbsp;
background I should be assuming from those of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:17.840
you watching right now. Maybe it's the case&nbsp;
that you're quite comfortable with calculus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:21.760
and you don't need me to belabor the point here.&nbsp;
Maybe it's the case that you're not familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:25.840
calculus, and I shouldn't just be throwing down&nbsp;
integrals like that. Or maybe you...you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:29.760
you took a calculus class a while ago&nbsp;
but you need a little bit of a refresher.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:33.360
I'm gonna go with the option of setting&nbsp;
this up as if it's a calculus lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:35.600
because to be honest even when&nbsp;
you are quite comfortable with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:38.560
integrals setting them up can be&nbsp;
kind of an error-prone process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:42.880
and calling back to the underlying definition is a&nbsp;
good way to sort of check yourself in the process.

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:47.360
If we lived in a time before calculus&nbsp;
existed and integrals weren't a thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:52.080
and we wanted to approximate an answer to this&nbsp;
question, one way we could go about it is to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:57.440
a sample of values for theta that ranges&nbsp;
from 0 up to 180 degrees. We might think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:01.840
of them as evenly spaced, with some sort of&nbsp;
difference between each one, some delta-theta.

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:06.800
And it's still the case that it would be unhelpful&nbsp;
to ask about the probability of a particular value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:11.200
of theta occurring, even if it's one in our&nbsp;
sample. That probability would still be zero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:16.480
and it would be unhelpful. But what is helpful&nbsp;
to ask is the probability of falling between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:21.520
different values from our sample, in this little&nbsp;
band of latitude with a width of delta theta.

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:26.240
Based on our assumption that the distribution&nbsp;
along the sphere should be uniform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:30.960
that probability comes down to knowing the area&nbsp;
of this band. More specifically, the chances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:35.840
that a randomly chosen vector lands in that band&nbsp;
should be that area divided by the total surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:40.720
area of the sphere. To figure out that area,&nbsp;
let's first think of the radius of that band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:45.840
which if the radius of our sphere is 1 is&nbsp;
definitely going to be smaller than 1. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:50.320
in fact, if we draw the appropriate little right&nbsp;
triangle here, you can see that that little radius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:55.520
let's just say at the top of the band should be&nbsp;
the sine of our angle, the sine of theta. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.520 --> 00:27:00.640
means that the circumference of the band should&nbsp;
be 2 pi times the sine of that angle. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:05.920
the area of the band should be that circumference&nbsp;
times its thickness, that little delta theta. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:11.040
rather, the area of our band is approximately this&nbsp;
quantity. What's important is that for a finer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:16.160
sample of many more values of theta the accuracy&nbsp;
of that approximation would get better and better.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:22.240
Now remember, the reason we wanted this area is&nbsp;
to know the probability of falling into that band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:27.040
which is this area divided by the surface area&nbsp;
of the sphere, which we know to be 4 pi times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:31.840
its radius squared. That's a value that you could&nbsp;
also compute with an integral similar to the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:36.000
that we're setting up now, but for now we can take&nbsp;
it as a given, as a standard well-known formula.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:40.880
And this probability itself is just a stepping&nbsp;
stone in the direction of what we actually want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:43.280
which is the average area&nbsp;
for the shadow of a square.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:49.200
To get that we'll multiply this probability times&nbsp;
the corresponding shadow area, which is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:52.880
absolute value of cosine theta expression&nbsp;
we've seen many times up to this point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:57.600
And our estimate for this average would&nbsp;
now come down to adding up this expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:01.840
across all of the different bands, all of the&nbsp;
different samples of theta that we've taken.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.320
This right here, by the way, is when&nbsp;
Bob is just totally in his element.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:09.680
We've got a lot of exact formulas&nbsp;
describing something very concrete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:14.800
actually digging in on our way to a real answer.&nbsp;
And again, if it feels like a lot of detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:19.520
I want you to appreciate that fact so that you&nbsp;
can appreciate just how magical it is when Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:24.800
manages to somehow avoid all of this. Anyway,&nbsp;
looking back at our expression, let's clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:28.880
things up a little bit, like factoring out all&nbsp;
of the terms that don't depend on theta itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:35.200
And we can simplify that 2 pi divided by 4 pi to&nbsp;
simply be one half. And to make it a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:39.680
analogous to calculus with integrals, let me&nbsp;
just swap the main terms inside the sum here.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:43.920
What we now have, this sum that's going&nbsp;
to approximate the answer to our question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:49.600
is almost what an integral is. Instead of writing&nbsp;
the sigma for sum, we write the integral symbol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:54.880
this kind of elongated Leibnizian S showing us&nbsp;
that we're going from zero to pi. And instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.880 --> 00:29:00.160
of describing the step size as delta theta, a&nbsp;
concrete finite amount, we instead describe it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:05.040
"d" theta, which I like to think of as signaling&nbsp;
the fact that some kind of limit is being taken.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:11.680
What that integral means, by definition, is&nbsp;
whatever the sum on the bottom approaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:16.800
for finer and finer subdivisions, more dense&nbsp;
samples that we might take for theta itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:23.040
And at this point, for those of you who do know&nbsp;
calculus, I'll just write down the details of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:28.080
you would actually carry this out as you might see&nbsp;
it written down in Bob's notebook. It's the usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:32.160
anti-derivative stuff, but the one key step&nbsp;
is to bring in a certain trig identity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:37.760
In the end, what Bob finds after doing&nbsp;
this is the surprisingly clean fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:43.440
that the average area for a square's shadow&nbsp;
is precisely one-half the area of that square.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:47.360
This is the mystery constant&nbsp;
which Alice doesn't yet know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.840
If Bob were to look over her shoulder and see&nbsp;
the work that she's done he could finish out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:56.000
the problem right now. He plugs in the constant&nbsp;
that he just found and he knows the final answer.

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:03.280
And now, finally! With all of this as backdrop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:06.560
what is it that Alice does to&nbsp;
carry out the final solution?

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:10.560
I introduced her as someone who really&nbsp;
likes to generalize the results she finds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:15.040
And usually those generalizations end up as&nbsp;
interesting footnotes that aren't really material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:20.480
for solving particular problems. But this is a&nbsp;
case where the generalization itself draws her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:26.080
a quantitative result. Remember, the substance of&nbsp;
what she's found so far is that if you look at any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:31.920
convex solid, then the average area for its shadow&nbsp;
is going to be proportional to its surface area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:37.920
And critically, it'll be the same proportionality&nbsp;
constant across all of these solids. So all Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:43.760
needs to do is find just a single convex solid out&nbsp;
there where she already knows the average area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:49.360
its shadow. And some of you may see where this is&nbsp;
going, the most symmetric solid available to us is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:54.960
a sphere. No matter what the orientation of that&nbsp;
sphere, its shadow, the flat projection shadow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.960 --> 00:31:00.960
is always a circle with an area of pi r squared.&nbsp;
So in particular that's its average shadow area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:06.320
And the surface area of a sphere, like I&nbsp;
mentioned before, is exactly 4 pi r squared.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:10.480
By the way, I did make a video talking&nbsp;
all about that surface area formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.480 --> 00:31:15.040
and how Archimedes proved it thousands of years&nbsp;
before calculus existed. So you don't need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:19.760
integrals to find it. The magic of what Alice&nbsp;
has done is that she can take this seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:25.120
specific fact, that the shadow of a sphere has&nbsp;
an area exactly one-fourth its surface area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:29.440
and use it to conclude a much more general&nbsp;
fact, that for any convex solid out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:32.480
its shadow and surface area&nbsp;
are related in the same way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:37.600
in a certain sense. Wo with that she can go and&nbsp;
fill in the details of the particular question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:43.120
about a cube and say that its average shadow area&nbsp;
will be one-fourth times its surface area, 6s^2.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:47.280
But the much more memorable fact that&nbsp;
she'll go to sleep thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:50.400
is how it didn't really matter that&nbsp;
we were talking about a cube at all.

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:57.040
Now, that's all very pretty, but some of you might&nbsp;
complain that this isn't really a valid argument,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.040 --> 00:32:03.440
because spheres don't have flat faces. When I said&nbsp;
Alice's argument generalizes to any convex solid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:07.760
if we actually look at the argument itself,&nbsp;
it definitely depends on the use of a finite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:11.680
number of flat faces. For example, if&nbsp;
we were mapping it to a dodecahedron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:15.920
you would start by saying that the area of&nbsp;
a particular shadow of that dodokahedron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:20.720
looks like exactly one half times the sum of&nbsp;
the areas of the shadows of all its faces.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:24.160
Snce again you could use a certain&nbsp;
ray-of-light-mixed-with-convexity argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:30.320
to draw that conclusion. And remember the benefit&nbsp;
of expressing that shadow area as a sum is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:35.840
when we want to average over a bunch of different&nbsp;
