00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:40.640
where we can then go column-by-column and consider&nbsp;
the average area for the shadow of each face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:46.080
And also, a critical fact was the conclusion from&nbsp;
much earlier that the average shadow for any 2d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:51.360
object (a flat 2d object, which is important)&nbsp;
will equal some universal proportionality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:56.080
constant times its area. The significance was that&nbsp;
that constant didn't depend on the shape itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.080 --> 00:33:00.800
it could have been a square, or a cat, or the&nbsp;
pentagonal faces of our dodecahedron, whatever.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:07.040
So after hastily carrying this over to a sphere&nbsp;
that doesn't have a finite number of flat faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:11.440
you would be right to complain. But luckily,&nbsp;
it's a pretty easy detail to fill in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:17.120
What you can do is imagine a sequence of different&nbsp;
polyhedra that successively approximate a sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:21.120
in the sense that their faces hug tighter and&nbsp;
tighter around the genuine surface of the sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:26.640
For each one of those approximations, we can draw&nbsp;
the same conclusion that its average shadow is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:31.360
going to be proportional to its surface area, with&nbsp;
this universal proportionality constant. So then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:36.720
if we say "okay, let's take the limit of the&nbsp;
ratio between the average shadow area at each step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:41.440
and the surface area at each step..." Well, since&nbsp;
that ratio is never changing, it's always equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:46.400
this constant, then in the limit it's also going&nbsp;
to equal that constant. But on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:51.360
by their definition, in the limit their average&nbsp;
shadow area should be that of a circle which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:56.400
pi r squared, and the limit of the surface areas&nbsp;
would be the surface area of the sphere, 4 pi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.400 --> 00:34:02.240
r squared. So we do genuinely get the conclusion&nbsp;
that intuition would suggest, but as is so common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:06.960
with Alice's argument here, we do have to be a&nbsp;
little delicate in how we justify that intuition.

